## R Function

R has a function for $\chi^2$-tests, `chisq.test(x)`. This function wants counted data, either a numeric vector of counts, or a `table` of the data.

:::: {.columns}
::: {.column .fragment width="33%"}

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
counted_major_data <- major_data |>
  count(major)
counted_major_data
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
# A tibble: 5 × 2
  major               n
  <chr>           <int>
1 Arts               32
2 Business           52
3 Humanities         32
4 STEM               51
5 Social Sciences    33
```


:::
:::

:::

::: {.column .fragment width="66%"}

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
tabled_major_data <- major_data |>
  select(major) |>
  table()
tabled_major_data
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
major
           Arts        Business      Humanities Social Sciences            STEM 
             32              52              32              33              51 
```


:::
:::

:::
::::

:::: {.columns}
::: {.column .fragment width="40%"}

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
chisq <- counted_major_data |>
  select(-major) |>
  chisq.test()
chisq
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```

	Chi-squared test for given probabilities

data:  select(counted_major_data, -major)
X-squared = 11.05, df = 4, p-value = 0.02601
```


:::
:::


:::

::: {.column .fragment width="60%"}

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
chisq <- tabled_major_data |>
  chisq.test()
chisq
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```

	Chi-squared test for given probabilities

data:  tabled_major_data
X-squared = 11.05, df = 4, p-value = 0.02601
```


:::
:::


